  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "One violin sold for 15.9 million dollars in 2011. The Lady Blunt, built by Antonio Stradivari in Cremona in 1721.",
        "visual_query": "cremona italy old street",
        "visual_fallback": "italian street"
      },
      {
        "narration": "He made over eleven hundred instruments. Around 650 still exist, and people have been chasing their secret for three straight centuries.",
        "visual_query": "violin workshop luthier hands",
        "visual_fallback": "violin workshop"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Dense wood from the Little Ice Age? Borax soaked in against woodworm? A varnish nobody could copy? Every theory got tested properly.",
        "visual_query": "alpine spruce forest winter",
        "visual_fallback": "snowy forest"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 2010 the varnish came back as ordinary resin, oil and pigment. Nothing secret in it. So where is the magic hiding?",
        "visual_query": "antique violin varnish closeup",
        "visual_fallback": "violin closeup"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 2014, ten soloists played twelve violins blind. Six picked a modern one, and guessing old from new came out roughly a coin flip. Thirty-one right, thirty-three wrong.",
        "visual_query": "concert hall violin soloist",
        "visual_fallback": "concert stage"
      }
    ],
